# Butterfly Project Guidelines

## Component Structure

- Components should be organized in `src/components/` by feature or type.
- Each component should have its own directory containing the component file (e.g., `Button.tsx`), its SCSS module (e.g., `_button.module.scss`), and potentially a test file.

## Styling

- Use SCSS modules for component-specific styling (`.module.scss`).
- Global styles should be defined in `src/styles/`.
- Variables and mixins should be defined in `src/styles/_variables.scss` and `src/styles/_mixins.scss` respectively.

## Image and Asset Management

- All images should be stored in `src/assets/images/`.
- All icons should be stored in `src/assets/icons/`.
- Use the `next/image` component for optimized image loading when appropriate.

## Data Fetching

- Prefer server-side rendering (SSR) or static site generation (SSG) with Next.js data fetching methods (`getStaticProps`, `getServerSideProps`).
- Client-side data fetching should use `SWR` or `React Query` for caching and state management.

## Accessibility

- Ensure all interactive elements have appropriate ARIA attributes.
- Provide `alt` text for all images.
- Keyboard navigation should be fully functional.

## Performance

- Optimize image sizes and formats.
- Implement lazy loading for offscreen images and components.
- Minimize bundle size by code splitting.

## Internationalization

- Use a consistent internationalization library (e.g., `next-i18next`) for all user-facing text.
- Store translations in dedicated JSON files.

## Naming Conventions

- React components should use PascalCase (e.g., `MyComponent.tsx`).
- SCSS module files should use kebab-case with a leading underscore (e.g., `_my-component.module.scss`).
- Variables and functions in JavaScript/TypeScript should use camelCase.

## Error Handling

- Implement robust error boundaries for React components.
- Log errors to a centralized service.
- Provide user-friendly error messages.

## Environment Variables

- Manage sensitive information using environment variables (`.env.local`).
- Access environment variables through `process.env.VAR_NAME` (for client-side, prefix with `NEXT_PUBLIC_`).
